# Code owners.
#
# A pull request that changes any file below needs a review from its owner. This only has an
# effect when the branch ruleset turns on "Require review from Code Owners". policies/security.yaml
# (repository_requirements.require_code_owner_review_for_protected_paths) requires that setting.
#
# What is listed here: the files that decide what "the checks passed" means, who may review, and
# which rules apply. They are the groups of protected paths in policies/security.yaml that are
# marked deny_write: ci_workflows, gate_and_toolchain_config, factory_config,
# agent_instruction_files, vcs_internals_and_hooks and factory_self_protection. A pull request
# that quietly weakens a check, edits this file, or changes a policy must not merge unseen.
#
# Owners: @ortisan has admin access to this repository today (checked with the GitHub API).
# The organization has no team yet. When one exists, replace the handle with the team, for
# example @ortisun/maintainers, and add a second owner. With a single owner, GitHub cannot
# count the owner's approval of their own pull request, so a protected change by the only
# owner has no second reviewer until another owner is added.
#
# The last matching line wins. Paths that start with / are relative to the repository root.

# --- CI definitions and this file itself (ci_workflows) -------------------------------------
/.github/                              @ortisan
/CODEOWNERS                            @ortisan
/docs/CODEOWNERS                       @ortisan

# --- The one script every check runs through, and where later packages add steps ------------
